    not_duplicate_of: []  # 8383
    pr_ref:  # 8383
    description: "**Symptom.** The auto-emitted budget-tier sketches (`behavior_map.4k.json`, `.16k.json`, `.64k.json`) and the `compact` view are not reproducible across Python hash seeds: for a fixed repo, the selected node SET (on tie-heavy graphs) and the node ORDER differ between `PYTHONHASHSEED` values.\n\n**Empirical confirmation (projection:F1 design recon, 2026-06-14).**\n\n*Unit probe* — star graph, one seed + 8 leaves all tied on the full score tuple `(component_growth=1, edges_added=1, centrality=0.5)`, `select_by_connectivity(..., max_additional=4)`:\n- `PYTHONHASHSEED=0` -> `['seed_node','alpha','hotel','golf','charlie']`\n- `PYTHONHASHSEED=1` -> `['seed_node','echo','hotel','charlie','foxtrot']`\n- `=2/3/42` each select a different 4-of-8 subset and order.\n\n*Whole-artifact probe* — `hypergumbo run packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core --budgets 4k,16k` under seed 0 vs 1:\n- `4k.json`: 20834 (s0) vs 20742 (s1) bytes; node SET differs (s0 `cmd_dead_code_maybe`, s1 `cmd_slice` — a genuine tie-broken swap).\n- `16k.json`: node set identical but ORDER diverges at index 5.\n- **Same-seed control** (seed 0 twice): node SET and ORDER identical (only `generated_at` / per-run `origin_run_id` differ) — proving the cross-seed difference is attributable to PYTHONHASHSEED, not run noise.\n\n**Leak sites (compact.py).**\n- **PRIMARY** `:718` `for node_id in frontier:` (a `set`) with strict-max `if score > best_score` at `:722` keeps the first tied node in set-iteration order. Reaches the artifact via `conn_result.included.symbols -> tiered_map[\"nodes\"]` (`:1451`, `:1520`) with NO final re-sort.\n- `:677` `for sid in seed_ids:` and `:703-709` `for sid in selected_ids:` (sets) seed/frontier-build in set order.\n- `:889` `for sid in force_include_ids:` (set, compact coverage branch) appends with no sort.\n- Ruled out (NOT leaks): `Counter.most_common` (insertion-stable on ties), `select_by_tokens` force-include (`.sort(key=(-centrality,name))`), the cross-cutting cap (`sorted(...,key=(-cc,x))`), the `tiered_map = {k:v}` dict-comp (insertion-ordered).\n\n**Fix direction.** Make the frontier tie-break total: `if score > best_score or (score == best_score and (best_node is None or node_id < best_node))`, or extend the score tuple with a final `node_id` tiebreaker and select via `max` over a stably-ordered frontier. Closure evidence: a `PYTHONHASHSEED=0/1` subprocess determinism test asserting byte-identical tiered/compact artifacts.\n\n**Why this is its own item (not folded into INV-pazur).** INV-pazur is about summary↔array internal consistency (closed by construction via the post-shrink summary re-derive, no behavior change, no bakeoff needed). This is a *distinct* invariant — artifact reproducibility across hash seeds — whose fix changes a bakeoff-tuned hot loop (`select_by_connectivity` carries keycloak-fragmentation / iceberg-0-edges tuning). Changing the tie-break changes WHICH nodes are selected on ties => shifts corpus outputs => the fix PR must carry `awaits_bakeoff_validation` and a bakeoff confirming selection quality held. Lexicographic `node_id` tie-break is deterministic but not obviously quality-neutral (could bias toward alphabetically-early paths), which is exactly why it needs corpus validation rather than a same-PR fold.\n\n**Distinct from WI-juhun** (positive control: the *semantic graph* — main `nodes`/`edges`/`stable_id`/`run_signature` — IS reproducible across runs). That holds; this defect is confined to the *projection* layer (budget-tier + compact node selection), which WI-juhun does not cover.\n\n**Discovered:** projection:F1 (`re_derive_view`/INV-pazur) design recon, workflow wf_ac67b907-937."  # 8383
    fields:  # 8383
      statement: "For a fixed repo, the emitted budget-tier (behavior_map.<budget>.json) and compact node SET and ORDER must be identical across PYTHONHASHSEED values. Currently select_by_connectivity's frontier tie-break makes both vary on tie-heavy graphs."  # 8383
      root_cause: "select_by_connectivity (compact.py:718) iterates `for node_id in frontier` (a set) and keeps the first strict-max (`if score > best_score`); on a (component_growth, edges_added, centrality) score tie the winner is set-iteration-order-dependent (PYTHONHASHSEED-dependent). conn_result.included.symbols is written straight to tiered/compact nodes in selection order with no final re-sort, so both the selected set (on real ties) and node order leak into the artifact."  # 8383
